- The `ClassUtils.getPackageName(Class<?>)` method retrieves the package name using the class name.

### Potential Fix
To address the issue reported in the bug where a semicolon is added to the end of class names for arrays, a modification is required in the `getShortClassName(Class)` method in the `ClassUtils` class. The fix should handle array class names appropriately to avoid appending a semicolon.

#### Suggested Fix
```java
// Inside ClassUtils.java
public static String getShortClassName(Class<?> cls) {
    if (cls == null) {
        return StringUtils.EMPTY;
    }

    String className = cls.getName();

    // Handle array class names without adding a semicolon
    if (cls.isArray()) {
        // Modify the logic here to get the short class name for arrays
        return getArrayComponentName(cls.getComponentType()) + "[]";
    }

    // Existing logic for non-array classes
    int lastDotIdx = className.lastIndexOf(PACKAGE_SEPARATOR);
    int nameEndIdx = className.length();
    return className.substring(lastDotIdx + 1, nameEndIdx);
}

private static String getArrayComponentName(Class<?> componentType) {
    // Recursive method to get the component type name for arrays
    // Handle nested arrays if necessary
    if (componentType.isArray()) {
        return getArrayComponentName(componentType.getComponentType()) + "[]";
    }
    return getShortClassName(componentType); // Get the short class name for the component type
}
```

This modification should ensure that the `getShortClassName` method in `ClassUtils` correctly handles array class names without adding an extra semicolon.

Please apply this fix in the `ClassUtils.getShortClassName` method to resolve the reported issue.

